{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Even not to a land, etc.</b> It is difficult: after they said 'to kill us in the wilderness,' what place is there to complain about a lesser degree than that? And Rashi, of blessed memory, resolves it with difficulty. Perhaps, however, the intention is to say that their denial of going to him was not on account of the evil outcome they had received from him — that he brought them out to kill them in the wilderness — but even on account of the absence of the good promised from him alone. And that is his saying <b>even not to</b>, etc. — meaning, even with respect to 'not to a land,' etc. For this reason alone — 'the eyes of the men,' etc. — and all the more so since he caused them the onset of harm." "<b>The eyes of, etc. ... we will not go up.</b> Meaning: they choose the gouging out of their eyes from on high, which they might have been assured of from him, over the good. In this they revealed the extent of the intensity of the hatred — that they hated the beneficence of the righteous man more than the gouging out of their eyes. Therefore the anger of Moses burned exceedingly."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:05.919003908Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}